{"name":"Water Wings","hex":"#80d5cc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water wings","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of water wing"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water_wings"}],"entries":[{"title":"Inflatable armbands","blocks":[{"text":"Inflatable armbands, usually referred to as simply armbands, water wings, swimmies, or floaties, are swim aids designed to help a wearer float in water and learn to swim."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Lucas_in_the_pool.jpg/330px-Lucas_in_the_pool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflatable_armbands"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ball-and-stick model of a water molecule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater-3D-balls-A.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/1280px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A drop of water falling toward water in a glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_drop_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/1280px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This pillow basalt on the seafloor near Hawaii was formed when magma extruded underwater. Other, much older pillow basalt formations provide evidence for large bodies of water long ago in Earth's history.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/500px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/1280px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png/500px-Water_density_volume_mass_metric.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ways of presenting the density of water using five metric units of length, volume, and mass.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_density_volume_mass_metric.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png/330px-Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png/330px-Tetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tetrahedral structure of water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATetrahedral_Structure_of_Water.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg/500px-Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg/500px-Heat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Specific heat capacity of water[102]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeat_capacity_of_water_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Water_cycle.png/500px-Water_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Water_cycle.png/500px-Water_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Water cycle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_cycle.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg/120px-Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg/250px-Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg/120px-Bay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABay_of_Fundy_High_Tide.jpg"}],"sounds":{}}}